# width: 120
# height: 34
# Help → Welcome (`view.welcome`) shows the start surface — the one
# welcome — on a fresh empty tab page, with the keys, and its SHORTCUTS
# are the active profile's chords read off the spec table: NvChad's under
# vim (Ctrl+N toggles the tree, never VS Code's Ctrl+B), VS Code's under
# standard. There is no second, hard-coded chord box.
command editor.use_vim
write a.txt "alpha\n"
open a.txt
command view.welcome
expect screen contains "SHORTCUTS"
expect screen contains " START "
expect screen contains "Ctrl+N     toggle the tree"
expect screen lacks "Ctrl+B"
expect screen lacks "The chords to start with"
expect screen lacks "ctrl+f"
# The file is still open on the first tab page.
command tab.prev
expect screen contains "1 alpha"
command editor.use_standard
command view.welcome
expect screen contains "toggle the tree"
expect screen contains "Ctrl+B  "
